issue_context.body:
Currently if we need to reject a request from a client before upgrading the WebSocket connection, we write a message to the socket and then destroy it. However when trying to use browser.connect in this scenario, we get the following error from the client

Upstream Issue - https://github.com/websockets/ws/issues/2080

#### Current Browser.Connect Message
```
    browserType.connect: WebSocket error: Unexpected server response: 429
    =========================== logs ===========================
    <ws connecting> ws://localhost:8080
    <ws error> [object Object]
    <ws connect error> ws://localhost:8080 Unexpected server response: 429
    <ws disconnected> ws://localhost:8080 code=1006 reason=
    ============================================================
```

#### Current Error Message when only error event is handled
```ts
const Websocket = require('ws');

const client = new Websocket('ws://localhost:8080');

client.on('error', err => {
  console.error(err.message);
});
```

```
Unexpected server response: 429
```

#### Error Message when unexpected-response event is handled [Preferred since it allows us to send messages to clients]
```ts
const Websocket = require('ws');

const client = new Websocket('ws://localhost:8080');

client.on("unexpected-response", function (request, response) {
  response.setEncoding("utf-8");
  process.stdout.write(response.statusCode + " " + response.statusMessage + "\n");
  response.on("data", function (chunk) {
    process.stdout.write(chunk);
  });
});

client.on('error', err => {
  console.error(err.message);
});
```

```
429 Too Many Requests
Sorry, request terminated
```
#### Mock Playwright Server
```js
const { createServer } = require("http");

const server = createServer();

server.on("upgrade", function upgrade(request, socket) {
  socket.write("HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests\r\n\r\n");
  socket.write(`Sorry, request terminated \r\n`);
  socket.destroy();
});

server.listen(8080);
```
